screening out porn.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.190
And this is actually an example of authorization where I think you should

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:05.590
take a machine learning problem and break it into distinct steps.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:11.470
So one knob or one step is to figure out how to define a metric that

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:16.120
captures what you want to do.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:21.960
And I would worry separately about how to actually do well on this metric.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.920
So I think of the machine learning task as two distinct steps to use the

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.400
target analogy.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.090
The first step is to place the target.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.770
So define where you want to aim, and then it's a completely separate step.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.530
So this is one knob you could tune, which is how do you place the target.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:44.010
And it's a completely separate problem.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:48.570
Think of it as a separate knob to tune in terms of how to do well at this

00:06:48.570 --> 00:07:00.580
target or how to aim accurately or how to shoot at the target.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:06.300
And so defining a metric is step one, and you do something else for step two.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.580
So in terms of shooting at a target, maybe your learning algorithm is

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:14.180
optimizing some cost function that looks like this.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:22.950
When you're minimizing this, you know, some of losses on your training set.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:27.630
So one thing you could do is to also modify this in order to incorporate

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:31.510
these weights, and maybe you end up changing this normalization constant as well

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:34.470
as I guess one over a sum of WI.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:38.270
Again, the details of how you define J aren't important, but the point was with

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:43.030
the philosophy of orthogonization, think of placing the target as one step and

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:47.870
aiming and shooting at the target as a distinct step, which you do separately.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:52.190
So in other words, I encourage you to think of defining the metric as one step,

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:56.150
and then you can, and only after you've defined the metric, figure out how to do

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:59.470
well on that metric, which might be changing the cost function J that your

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:00.830
neural network is optimizing.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:03.670
Before going on, let's look at just one more example.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:10.470
Let's say that your two CAD classifiers, A and B, have respectively 3% error and 5% error

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:15.590
as evaluated on your dev set, or maybe even on your test set, which are

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:19.590
images downloaded off the internet, so high quality well-frame images.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.030
But maybe when you deploy your algorithm product, you find that algorithm B

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:26.430
actually looks like it's performing better, even though it's doing better

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.390
on your dev set.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:32.830
And you find that you've been training off very nice, high quality images

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:37.510
downloaded off the internet, but when you deploy this on a mobile app, users

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:38.830
are uploading all sorts of pictures.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:41.550
They're much less framed, you have only part of the CAD, the CAD is

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:45.070
funny facial expressions, maybe images are much blurrier.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:49.070
And when you test out your algorithms, you find that algorithm B is

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.990
actually doing better.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:58.870
So this would be another example of your metric and dev test set falling down.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:03.590
And the problem is that you're evaluating on a dev and test set, very nice,

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:05.870
high resolution, well-framed images.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.070
But what your users really care about is the algorithm doing well on

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:13.150
images they're uploading, which are maybe less professionally shot and

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:16.230
blurrier and less well-framed.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:20.630
So the guideline is, if doing well on your metric and your current

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:24.990
dev set or dev and test sets distribution, if that does not correspond

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.550
to doing well on the application you actually care about, then change

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:32.780
your metric and your dev test set.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.700
So in other words, if you discover that your dev test set has these

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:42.780
very high quality images, but evaluating on this dev test set is not

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:46.740
predictive of how well your app actually performs because your app needs

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.740
to deal with lower quality images, then that's a good time to change

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:55.060
your dev test set so that your data better reflects the type of data you

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.020
actually need to do well on.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:00.700
But your overall guideline is really, if your current metric and data you're

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.340
evaluating on doesn't correspond to doing well on what you actually care

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.860
about, then change your metric and your dev test set to better capture

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:11.380
what you need the algorithm to actually do well on.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:15.060
Having the evaluation metric and the dev set allows you to much more

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.980
quickly make decisions about is algorithm A or algorithm B better, so it

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.460
really speeds up how quickly you or your team can iterate.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:27.060
So my recommendation is even if you can't define the perfect evaluation

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.900
metric and dev set, just set something up quickly and use that to drive

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.900
the speed of your team iterating.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.020
And later down the line, you find out that it wasn't a good one,

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:39.740
you have a better idea, change it at that time is perfectly okay.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:43.940
But what I recommend against for most teams is to run for too long

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:48.460
without an evaluation metric and dev set because that can slow down

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:52.780
the efficiency of which your team can iterate and improve your algorithm.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:57.900
So that's it on when to change your evaluation metric and or dev and

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:58.980
test sets.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.060
Hope that these guidelines help you set up your whole team to have a well

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:06.660
defined target that you can iterate efficiently toward improving

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:07.460
performance on.
